/*******************************************************************************
	Kant in kant: the plan
*******************************************************************************/
Goal: jitted turtles all the way down. rule() fires its parse; the parse fires
its action; the action BUILDS an instruct and returns it as its label. Running
the label runs the program. Proof that kant is a language that writes itself.

/*******************************************************************************
	Two phases, never mixed
*******************************************************************************/
Build time: a rule action fires during the parse and builds an instruct. It
evaluates nothing (bear-trap #34: building must never fire).
The one exception: code that DECIDES the parse (accept or reject a match) runs
at parse time, because the parse cannot wait for it.
Until kant can write a rejection (open question 1), a rule's parse decision
may live on its own small rule beneath it, in C++. The worked example:
    SHORTCUTs=[-+~`$_:,]+ ruleMethod=shortcutTest;   decides (rejects ++ -- $$)
    SHORTCUt  shortcut=SHORTCUTs;                    builds (kant action)
Run time: the instruct runs. testOP is every instruct's method: if a field has
a method, run it; if not, hand it back as is. A plain value is a valid chain end.

/*******************************************************************************
	The shapes
*******************************************************************************/
Expression chain   op target argument, a tree (EXPRESSIOn builds these today).
seq instruct       op = seq, argument = a LIST of statement instructs.
                   opSeq walks the list in order. An action body is a seq.
                   Built.
print instruct     op = printList, argument = the list of items. A shortcut
                   item is its own instruct, op = printShortcut. Built.
if instruct        condition chain, then chain, else chain. opIf runs the
                   condition and picks one.
loop instruct      body (a seq) and condition. Reruns while the condition holds.
Every value-producing instruct hands back its result on its own node, never a
shared slot. Today's exceptions, each on purpose until IF's recon rules on them:
    opPrintList ends in opPrint and hands back trueResult (the shared sentinel);
    opSeq hands back its argument list;
    opPrintShortcut hands back null, so the print loop skips it as an item.
IF's recon decides what an if instruct hands back, and whether a seq does too.

/*******************************************************************************
	Branches: signals, not targets
*******************************************************************************/
break, continue, return raise a signal (branchKind). Nobody computes a target.
The nearest loop instruct reads break and continue after each statement;
the action's own seq reads return.

/*******************************************************************************
	Frames
*******************************************************************************/
processAction keeps its bracket: each activation's locals saved and restored.
Only its body walk changes, to "run the chain". The one engine change the
plan needs, beside the new ops (opSeq, opIf, the loop op).

/*******************************************************************************
	The new grammar
*******************************************************************************/
A new incantation beside grammar (kantGrammar). How it is reached today: a file
registers it in fILEs and includes it (printFormatT's prelude is the pattern),
and a new rule runs by being called by name, rule("text") (R6). Swapping
Grokking out of the search list changes name lookups, not the driver (seal 234),
so that swap is dropped for now.
Setup, the bootstrap and anything already compiled stay on Grokking. No change
to existing code or interpreter.
Every new rule action is kant and returns an instruct, except a parse decision
(see Two phases).
Rules for every new rule:
    - No meaning carried in C++ flags. If an item means something, that meaning
      is an instruct, an op or an attribute, never a bit the engine remembers.
    - Resolve operands at run time, never at build.
    - A built instruct holds copies (copyOf), never the action's own locals: a
      local is per-call scratch, so an instruct that kept one would read the
      last call's value (bear-trap 50; PRINt's itemsMint, SHORTCUt's scText).
    - A parse decision cannot share a rule with the build: ruleMethod= on a
      rule displaces its code={} (measured, SEQ 462). The decider goes on its
      own small rule beneath, as SHORTCUTs.

/*******************************************************************************
	The ladder: one rung at a time, each checked against the old grammar
*******************************************************************************/
Every rung runs the same incantation under both grammars and compares output.
    1  two prints in a seq             statements in a chain        DONE
    2  EXPRESSIOn (EXPRESSIOn, R0)    expressions in the new grammar  DONE
       PRINt finished alongside: formats and shortcuts DONE; left: adjacent
       expressions (open question 4) and the quoted-print row (R7)
    3  IF                              build an if instruct; then / else
                                       NEXT once PRINt's two leftovers land
    4  a loop (while or DO)            seq + branch signals: break, continue
    5  an action, called and self-called    the frame bracket
    6  the jitter walks the chain      later

/*******************************************************************************
	Jitting
*******************************************************************************/
The list is how the program is held, not how it runs. Interpreted: testOP walks
the list. Jitted: the jitter walks it once at compile time and emits straight-
line code; seq flattens, if becomes a branch, a loop a back-edge, break a jump.
First step, before any emitter: a recon of the new ops under jitter;, measured
against the rules for new rules. Today the new ops hand over to run time.
A split rule (decide below, build above) adds one label level; check it there.

